"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":["from tokenizers import Tokenizer\n","from tokenizers.models import BPE\n","from tokenizers.trainers import BpeTrainer\n","from tokenizers.pre_tokenizers import Whitespace\n","\n","tokenizer = Tokenizer(BPE(unk_token=\"[UNK]\"))\n","\n","trainer = BpeTrainer(\n","    vocab_size=100,\n","    special_tokens=[\"[UNK]\"]\n",")\n","\n","tokenizer.pre_tokenizer = Whitespace()"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":13,"id":"83c3cb6d","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-08T03:53:55.48428Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-08T03:53:55.483948Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-08T03:53:55.488571Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-08T03:53:55.487722Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.017103,"end_time":"2026-11-08T03:53:55.490426+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-08T03:53:55.473323+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":["texts = [\n","    \"The cat sat on the mat because it was tired.\",\n","    \"The cat was tired after sitting on the mat.\",\n","    \"The mat was where the cat sat.\"\n","]"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":14,"id":"6e50372b","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-08T03:53:55.511338Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-08T03:53:55.510668Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-08T03:53:55.542263Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-08T03:53:55.540848Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.045302,"end_time":"2026-11-08T03:53:55.545025+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-08T03:53:55.499723+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["\n","\n","\n"]}],"source":["tokenizer.train_from_iterator(texts, trainer)\n","output = tokenizer.encode(text)\n","tokens = output.tokens"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":15,"id":"493a77fc","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-08T03:53:55.566695Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-08T03:53:55.566353Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-08T03:53:55.571532Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-08T03:53:55.570608Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.017938,"end_time":"2026-11-08T03:53:55.573583+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-08T03:53:55.555645+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Encoder: Encoding(num_tokens=11, attributes=[ids, type_ids, tokens, offsets, attention_mask, special_tokens_mask, overflowing])\n","\n","Original text: The cat sat on the mat because it was tired.\n","Tokens: ['The', 'cat', 'sat', 'on', 'the', 'mat', 'because', 'it', 'was', 'tired', '.']\n"]}],"source":["# Display results\n","print(\"Encoder:\", output)\n","print(\"\\nOriginal text:\", text)\n","print(\"Tokens:\", tokens)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"4e2524a0","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.009376,"end_time":"2026-11-08T03:53:55.5927+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-08T03:53:55.583324+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["The exact divisions depend on the tokenizer.\n","\n","Why is subword tokenization so popular?\n","\n","Consider these words:\n","\n","```\n","play\n","playing\n","played\n","player\n","playful\n","```\n","\n","A subword tokenizer can reuse the common piece `play` instead of requiring an entirely independent vocabulary entry for every variation. This provides a balance between word-based and character-based tokenization.\n","\n","## How do modern LLMs tokenize text internally\n","\n","Now let's understand three important algorithms used in modern tokenizers.\n","\n","### BPE (Byte Pair Encoding)\n","\n","Important for LLM interviews. It is a subword tokenization algorithm that builds a vocabulary by repeatedly merging frequently occurring adjacent units.\n","\n","Let's understand it step by step.\n","\n","Suppose our training dataset contains:\n","\n","```\n","low\n","lower\n","lowest\n","newer\n","newest\n","```\n","\n","Step 1: Initially, divide words into characters.\n","\n","```\n","low   → l o w\n","lower → l o w e r\n","lowest → l o w e s t\n","newer → n e w e r\n","newest → n e w e s t\n","```\n","\n","Step 2: Count frequently occurring adjacent pairs.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","```\n","l + o\n","o + w\n","e + r\n","e + s\n","s + t\n","```\n","\n","Suppose `l + o` occurs most frequently.\n","\n","Step 3: Merge the most frequent pair.\n","\n","```\n","l + o → lo\n","```\n","\n","Now the vocabulary contains `lo` as a new token.\n","\n","Step 4: Repeat the process.\n","\n","Suppose `lo + w` is now the most frequent pair.\n","\n","```\n","lo + w → low\n","```\n","\n","Then another merge might be:\n","\n","```\n","e + r → er\n","```\n","\n","The algorithm keeps merging frequently occurring pairs until it reaches its target vocabulary size or another stopping condition.\n","\n","Step 5: Final tokenization\n","\n","The vocabulary may now contain:\n","\n","```\n","l\n","o\n","w\n","lo\n","low\n","er\n","est\n","new\n","...\n","```\n","\n","So the word:\n","\n","```\n","lowest\n","```\n","\n","might be tokenized as:\n","\n","```\n","[\"low\", \"est\"]\n","```\n","\n","This is a simplified demonstration. Actual BPE tokenization depends on the learned merge rules, initial alphabet, preprocessing, and tokenization configuration.\n","\n","**Where is BPE used?**\n","\n","- GPT-family tokenizers commonly use BPE variants.\n","- Many code and text language models use BPE-based tokenization.\n","\n","\n","**Why do we need `texts`?**\n","\n","Because a BPE tokenizer needs examples to learn how to split text into tokens. `texts` is your training dataset. It contains example sentences that the tokenizer learns from. Think of `texts` as the textbook from which the tokenizer learns its vocabulary.\n","\n","The tokenizer analyzes these sentences to learn:\n","\n","- Which words appear frequently.\n","- Which characters or groups of characters occur together.\n","- Which subword units can be combined.\n","- How to build its vocabulary.\n","\n","**What is the difference between training and encoding?**\n","\n","This is the most important concept.\n","\n","| Operation | Purpose |\n","| --- | --- |\n","| `texts` | Provides training examples |\n","| `tokenizer.train_from_iterator()` | Learns the vocabulary |\n","| `tokenizer.encode()` | Converts new text into tokens |\n","| `output.tokens` | Displays the resulting tokens |\n","| `output.ids` | Displays numerical IDs for tokens |\n","\n","**Why do we encode text?**\n","\n","Computers and machine learning models generally work with numerical representations rather than raw text.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","```text\n","The cat sat on the mat because it was tired.\n","```\n","\n","Might become:\n","\n","```python\n","[22, 24, 32, 31, 28, 25, 50, 30, 29, 34, 1]\n","```\n","\n","These numbers are called token IDs.\n","\n","Training builds the tokenizer's vocabulary, while encoding uses that vocabulary to convert sentences into numbers.\n","\n","### WordPiece\n","\n","It is associated with BERT and related models. Unlike basic BPE, WordPiece typically chooses merges based on how well they improve the likelihood of the training data under its vocabulary model.\n","\n","```\n","playing\n","```\n","\n","Could be divided into:\n","\n","```\n","[\"play\", \"##ing\"]\n","````\n","\n","**What does `##` mean?**\n","\n","It indicates that the subword is attached to the preceding token rather than beginning a new word.\n","\n","- For example: `unbelievable`\n","- Might become: `[\"un\", \"##believ\", \"##able\"]`\n","\n","The exact segmentation depends on the trained vocabulary.\n","\n","**Key Difference**\n","\n","- BPE: Uses frequency-based pair merging.\n","- WordPiece: Uses a likelihood-based criterion for selecting vocabulary pieces."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":16,"id":"c719c566","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-08T03:53:55.613691Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-08T03:53:55.61299Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-08T03:54:12.445893Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-08T03:54:12.444755Z"},"papermill":{"duration":16.84639